window.BioChecks[32] = {"2005-Q15a":[{"q":"Why is it hard to call a virus a living organism?","right":"It is not made of cells","wrong":["It contains both DNA and RNA","It has no genetic material","It reproduces by binary fission"],"why":"Viruses are non-cellular and can only reproduce inside a host cell, so they don't fit the usual rules for life."},{"q":"Which of these human diseases is caused by a virus?","right":"Measles","wrong":["Tuberculosis","Tetanus","Athlete's foot"],"why":"Measles is caused by a virus; TB and tetanus are bacterial, and athlete's foot is a fungus."},{"q":"What do B lymphocytes do when microbes get into the blood?","right":"Make antibodies against the antigen","wrong":["Engulf microbes by phagocytosis","Burst infected cells with perforin","Switch off the immune response"],"why":"B cells make antibodies; killer T cells burst infected cells and suppressor T cells switch the response off."},{"q":"Is vaccine immunity like immunity after an infection, and why?","right":"Yes: both make the body produce antibodies","wrong":["No: a vaccine gives ready-made antibodies","No: only an infection makes memory cells","Yes: both of them give passive immunity"],"why":"Both expose you to the antigen, so your own lymphocytes make antibodies and memory cells: active immunity."}],"2005-Q15b":[{"q":"What does it mean to call a bacterium pathogenic?","right":"It causes disease in its host","wrong":["It feeds on dead organic matter","It makes its own food","It is resistant to antibiotics"],"why":"Pathogenic means disease-causing; a bacterium that feeds on dead matter is a saprophyte."},{"q":"By natural selection, why is misusing antibiotics dangerous?","right":"Resistant bacteria survive and multiply","wrong":["Your body becomes immune to the drug","Each bacterium learns to resist the drug","The antibiotic destroys your antibodies"],"why":"Misuse kills the sensitive bacteria, so any resistant ones survive, multiply and build up a resistant population."}],"2006-Q6e":[{"q":"Which describes an antigen (not an antibody)?","right":"A foreign substance that triggers antibodies","wrong":["A protein made by lymphocytes to fight germs","A drug made by fungi that kills bacteria","A white blood cell that engulfs bacteria"],"why":"An antigen is the foreign molecule; the antibody is the protein your lymphocytes make in response to it."}],"2006-Q15b":[{"q":"What are antibiotics and what are they used for?","right":"Made by microbes; used to treat infections","wrong":["Made by lymphocytes; used to fight infections","Weakened microbes; used to give immunity","Made by the body; used to kill viruses"],"why":"Antibiotics are made by bacteria or fungi and used to treat infections; antibodies are what your own body makes."},{"q":"What is meant by antibiotic resistance?","right":"Bacteria are not killed by the antibiotic","wrong":["The patient becomes immune to the antibiotic","The antibiotic kills bacteria more quickly","Antibodies stop working on the bacteria"],"why":"Resistant bacteria survive the drug; it builds up by natural selection as resistant strains survive and multiply."}],"2006-Q15c":[{"q":"Which is a real biological benefit of breastfeeding?","right":"Passes antibodies to the baby","wrong":["Gives the baby active immunity","Makes ovulation return sooner","Stops the uterus contracting"],"why":"Milk passes on the mother's antibodies (passive immunity); it also delays ovulation and helps the uterus contract."}],"2007-Q14b":[{"q":"Which feature makes it hard to call viruses living?","right":"Can only reproduce inside a host cell","wrong":["Can reproduce alone by binary fission","Make their own energy by respiration","Have their own ribosomes and organelles"],"why":"Viruses are obligate parasites: they only reproduce inside a host cell and have no metabolism of their own."},{"q":"What two substances make up a virus particle?","right":"Protein and nucleic acid","wrong":["Lipid and carbohydrate","Cellulose and chitin","Starch and phospholipid"],"why":"A virus is just a protein coat around a core of nucleic acid, either DNA or RNA."},{"q":"Which of these is a viral disease?","right":"Polio","wrong":["Cholera","Ringworm","Tetanus"],"why":"Polio is caused by a virus; cholera and tetanus are bacterial, and ringworm is caused by a fungus."},{"q":"Which is a beneficial use of viruses?","right":"As vectors in genetic engineering","wrong":["Making antibiotics like penicillin","Making yoghurt and cheese","Fixing nitrogen in the soil"],"why":"Viruses can carry genes into cells, so they're used as vectors; penicillin comes from a fungus, not a virus."},{"q":"What is an antibiotic?","right":"A microbe-made chemical that kills bacteria","wrong":["A protein made by B cells against antigens","A harmless dose of a pathogen","A chemical the body makes to kill viruses"],"why":"Antibiotics are made by micro-organisms (bacteria or fungi) and kill or stop the growth of other micro-organisms."},{"q":"Why not give antibiotics for a viral infection?","right":"Antibiotics have no effect on viruses","wrong":["Antibiotics kill viruses too slowly","Antibiotics make the virus multiply","Viruses are killed by vaccines instead"],"why":"Antibiotics act on bacteria (and fungi), not viruses; giving them anyway just helps resistant bacteria build up."}],"2007-Q14c":[{"q":"What does the word immunity mean?","right":"Resistance to infection or disease","wrong":["Being more likely to catch an infection","Taking antibiotics to clear an infection","Having no microbes living on the body"],"why":"Immunity is the ability to resist infection, usually because antibodies and memory cells are ready for the antigen."},{"q":"What is the main job of B lymphocytes?","right":"Make antibodies against specific antigens","wrong":["Burst infected cells using perforin","Engulf bacteria by phagocytosis","Switch off the immune response"],"why":"B cells recognise a specific antigen and make antibodies (and memory cells); bursting infected cells is a killer T job."},{"q":"Which describes active immunity (not passive)?","right":"The body makes its own antibodies","wrong":["Antibodies are injected into the body","Antibodies pass from mother to baby","Antibiotics are taken to kill bacteria"],"why":"In active immunity your own lymphocytes make antibodies; in passive immunity you receive ready-made antibodies."},{"q":"Why does a vaccine give active immunity?","right":"Its antigens make the body produce antibodies","wrong":["It injects ready-made antibodies into you","It contains antibiotics that kill the germ","It adds donor white blood cells to the blood"],"why":"A vaccine brings in a harmless antigen, and your own lymphocytes respond by making antibodies: that's active."},{"q":"Being given a specific antibody is which type of immunity?","right":"Passive","wrong":["Active","Innate","Inherited"],"why":"The antibodies are made outside your body and given ready-made, so it's passive; active means your body makes them."},{"q":"When might an antibody be given instead of a vaccine?","right":"When infection may already have happened","wrong":["When lifelong protection is needed","When the person is already immune","When the patient is allergic to antibiotics"],"why":"Antibodies work at once, which helps if someone may already be infected and there's no time for a vaccine to work."},{"q":"How long does immunity from a given antibody last?","right":"Short: weeks to a few months","wrong":["Lifelong, like after an infection","Many years, like a vaccine","Longer than active immunity"],"why":"The given antibodies are broken down and no memory cells are made, so the protection soon fades."}],"2008-Q6d":[{"q":"Which is a biological advantage of breastfeeding?","right":"Milk is sterile and at the right temperature","wrong":["Milk contains antibiotics for the baby","It gives the baby active immunity","It makes ovulation return sooner"],"why":"Breast milk is sterile, at body temperature and full of antibodies; those give passive, not active, immunity."}],"2008-Q15b":[{"q":"Why did myxomatosis kill so many rabbits at first?","right":"The rabbits had no immunity to the virus","wrong":["The virus was resistant to antibiotics","The rabbits already had memory cells for it","The virus could multiply outside cells"],"why":"The virus was new to Ireland, so no rabbit had antibodies or memory cells, and fleas spread it fast."},{"q":"Why is myxomatosis less of a threat to Irish rabbits now?","right":"Resistant rabbits survived and bred","wrong":["All wild rabbits are now vaccinated","Antibiotics now cure the disease","The virus has died out in Ireland"],"why":"Rabbits with natural immunity survived and passed it on (natural selection); antibiotics don't work on viruses."},{"q":"Which is a sound reason for your answer on the HIV trend?","right":"Yes: resistant people survive and have children","wrong":["No: antibiotics will soon wipe out HIV","No: the HIV virus is unable to mutate","Yes: antibiotics will make people immune"],"why":"Natural selection works on humans too, just slowly; HIV is a virus that mutates, and antibiotics don't touch it."},{"q":"Which best describes how a virus replicates?","right":"Injects nucleic acid; host cell makes new viruses","wrong":["Splits in two by binary fission","Uses its own ribosomes to make protein","Forms spores that grow into new viruses"],"why":"A virus attaches, injects its DNA or RNA, and uses the host's ribosomes and enzymes to make and assemble new viruses."}],"2009-Q15c":[{"q":"Which matches a lymphocyte to its role?","right":"B cells make antibodies","wrong":["Killer T cells make antibodies","B cells burst infected cells","Helper T cells engulf bacteria"],"why":"B lymphocytes produce antibodies; T lymphocytes do jobs like killing infected cells or helping B cells."},{"q":"Which is an economic or medical effect of viruses?","right":"Tobacco mosaic virus damages crops","wrong":["Viruses make penicillin for medicine","Viruses fix nitrogen for farm crops","Viruses are used to brew beer"],"why":"Viruses cost money by harming crops and livestock; penicillin and brewing use fungi, and nitrogen fixing uses bacteria."}],"2010-Q6":[{"q":"What is part A on the diagram made of?","right":"Protein","wrong":["Lipid","Carbohydrate","Nucleic acid"],"why":"The coat and other outer parts of a virus are protein; the nucleic acid (part B) is the genetic material inside."},{"q":"What is part B on the diagram made of?","right":"Nucleic acid","wrong":["Protein","Lipid","Carbohydrate"],"why":"B is the virus's genetic material: a nucleic acid, either DNA or RNA, wrapped in the protein coat."},{"q":"After attaching, what does a virus put into the host cell?","right":"Its nucleic acid","wrong":["Its protein coat","Its own ribosomes","Its mitochondria"],"why":"The virus injects its nucleic acid, which then uses the host's ribosomes and materials to make new viruses."},{"q":"Why were older people less likely to get ill with swine flu?","right":"Earlier exposure left them with memory cells","wrong":["Older people have stronger general defences","They had taken more antibiotics over the years","Older people make more white blood cells"],"why":"Older people had probably met a similar flu virus before, so they had antibodies and memory cells ready."}],"2010-Q15c":[{"q":"Why won't doctors give antibiotics for a cold?","right":"A cold is viral; antibiotics don't affect viruses","wrong":["A cold is caused by a fungus, not bacteria","Antibiotics make the cold virus resistant","Antibiotics stop you making antibodies"],"why":"Colds are caused by viruses, which antibiotics can't touch; overusing them also breeds resistant bacteria."}],"2011-Q10b":[{"q":"Why might deaths from a virus fall over the years?","right":"Hosts develop immunity to it","wrong":["Antibiotics start to work on it","Hosts lose their antibodies to it","The virus stops being a parasite"],"why":"Over time the population builds immunity (natural selection favours resistant hosts), so fewer die."}],"2012-Q15b":[{"q":"How does a red blood cell differ from a white blood cell?","right":"It has no nucleus when mature","wrong":["It is larger than a white cell","There are fewer of them in blood","It makes antibodies against germs"],"why":"Mature red cells lose their nucleus to fit in more haemoglobin; they're also smaller and far more numerous."},{"q":"Which group of white blood cells is NOT a type of lymphocyte?","right":"Monocytes","wrong":["B cells","Killer T cells","Platelets"],"why":"Monocytes are phagocytes that engulf microbes; B and T cells are lymphocytes, and platelets aren't white cells."},{"q":"What do antibodies do in the body?","right":"Bind to antigens and inactivate them","wrong":["Engulf microbes by phagocytosis","Burst infected cells with perforin","Release antibiotics to kill bacteria"],"why":"Antibodies lock onto a specific antigen and inactivate it, for example by clumping or bursting the microbe."},{"q":"Which list has three types of T cell?","right":"Helper, killer, suppressor","wrong":["Helper, killer, plasma","Killer, memory, B cell","Helper, suppressor, phagocyte"],"why":"The four T cell types are helper, killer, suppressor and memory; B cells and phagocytes are different white cells."},{"q":"What is the role of suppressor T cells?","right":"Switch off the immune response","wrong":["Stimulate B cells to make antibodies","Burst infected cells with perforin","Remember antigens for later"],"why":"Suppressor T cells stop the response once the infection is over; helper T cells are the ones that stimulate B cells."}],"2013-Q12a":[{"q":"What evidence fits a virus being 'escaped genetic material'?","right":"It is made of nucleic acid (DNA or RNA)","wrong":["It has its own nucleus and membrane","It can respire and grow on its own","It is made of cellulose and lipid"],"why":"A virus is basically DNA or RNA in a protein coat, like a stray bit of a cell's genes."},{"q":"Why are viruses called obligate parasites?","right":"They can only reproduce inside a host cell","wrong":["They always kill their host straight away","They feed on dead and decaying matter","They can reproduce in or out of a cell"],"why":"'Obligate' means they have no choice: viruses can only reproduce using a living host cell's machinery."},{"q":"How can a virus be useful to people?","right":"Used to treat some cancers","wrong":["Used to make penicillin","Used to make yoghurt","Used to make bread rise"],"why":"Viruses are used in cancer treatment and genetic engineering; penicillin, yoghurt and bread need fungi or bacteria."}],"2013-Q12c":[{"q":"Which statement about antibodies and antibiotics is right?","right":"Antibodies are proteins made by the body","wrong":["Antibodies are made by fungi to kill bacteria","Antibiotics are made by B lymphocytes","Antibiotics kill viruses as well as bacteria"],"why":"Antibodies are your own proteins made against antigens; antibiotics are made by microbes and don't affect viruses."},{"q":"In passive immunity, where do the antibodies come from?","right":"They are given ready-made","wrong":["Your own B cells make them","Your memory T cells make them","A vaccine makes you produce them"],"why":"Passive means you receive antibodies made elsewhere (injection or breast milk); active means your body makes them."},{"q":"Why are infections easier to pick up in hospital?","right":"Antibiotic-resistant bacteria are common there","wrong":["Patients there have stronger immune systems","Antibiotics make patients' antibodies fail","Vaccines given there spread the disease"],"why":"Heavy antibiotic use in hospitals selects resistant strains (like MRSA), and patients are weaker and close together."}],"2014-Q15b":[{"q":"Why did Australia's rabbit numbers recover?","right":"Rabbits evolved immunity to the virus","wrong":["Antibiotics cured the infected rabbits","Farmers vaccinated the wild rabbits","The virus could not infect European rabbits"],"why":"The few rabbits with resistance survived and bred, so natural selection built up an immune population."}],"2015-Q3":[{"q":"What is part A of the virus mainly made of?","right":"Protein","wrong":["Carbohydrate","Nucleic acid","Cellulose"],"why":"Part A is made mainly of protein, like every virus coat; the nucleic acid (B) is the genetic material."},{"q":"What does 'obligate parasite' mean for a virus?","right":"It can only replicate using a living cell","wrong":["It can replicate inside or outside a cell","It must always kill the cell it enters","It lives on dead matter outside cells"],"why":"A virus has no machinery of its own, so it can only make copies of itself inside a host cell."},{"q":"Part B is one of two possible molecules. Which one?","right":"RNA","wrong":["Protein","Lipid","Carbohydrate"],"why":"Part B is the genetic material: either DNA or RNA, never both, inside the protein coat."},{"q":"Which virus causes disease in plants?","right":"Tobacco mosaic virus","wrong":["Influenza virus","Myxoma virus","Measles virus"],"why":"Tobacco mosaic virus blotches the leaves of plants; flu, measles and myxoma viruses infect animals."},{"q":"How do scientists tell different viruses apart?","right":"By the shape of their protein coat","wrong":["By the type of nucleus they have","By how they carry out respiration","By staining their cell walls"],"why":"Viruses differ in coat shape, size and type of nucleic acid; they have no nucleus, cell wall or respiration."},{"q":"Why don't viruses fit into any kingdom?","right":"They are not made of cells","wrong":["They contain both DNA and RNA","They are all prokaryotic cells","They make their own food"],"why":"Kingdoms group cellular organisms; viruses have no cells or organelles, so they don't fit."},{"q":"Which is one economic importance of viruses?","right":"Used to manufacture vaccines","wrong":["Used to brew beer and wine","Used to produce penicillin","Used to make biogas from waste"],"why":"Viruses are used to make vaccines and can harm crops and livestock; penicillin, beer and biogas use fungi or bacteria."}],"2015-Q14a":[{"q":"Which pairs a general defence with how it works?","right":"Mucus traps microbes; cilia sweep it away","wrong":["Skin makes antibodies to stop microbes","Phagocytes burst cells with perforin","Clotting releases antibodies into wounds"],"why":"Mucus in the airways traps microbes and cilia sweep them out; antibodies belong to the specific system."},{"q":"Which organ belongs to the immune system?","right":"Thymus","wrong":["Thyroid","Pancreas","Kidney"],"why":"The thymus is where T cells mature; the thyroid is a hormone gland in the neck, not an immune organ."},{"q":"What is an antibody?","right":"A protein made in response to an antigen","wrong":["A foreign molecule that triggers immunity","A chemical made by fungi to kill bacteria","A white blood cell that engulfs germs"],"why":"Antibodies are proteins made by B cells against a specific antigen; the antigen is the foreign molecule itself."},{"q":"What do killer T cells do?","right":"Burst infected cells using perforin","wrong":["Make antibodies against antigens","Switch off the immune response","Stimulate B cells to divide"],"why":"Killer T cells recognise infected or cancer cells and burst them with perforin; helper T cells stimulate B cells."},{"q":"Which T cells stop the immune response?","right":"Suppressor T cells","wrong":["Helper T cells","Killer T cells","Memory T cells"],"why":"Suppressor (regulatory) T cells switch the immune response off once the infection has been cleared."}],"2015-Q15c":[{"q":"What is a pathogen?","right":"Something that causes disease","wrong":["A chemical that kills bacteria","A protein that fights an infection","A microbe that breaks down dead matter"],"why":"A pathogen is anything that causes disease, like a disease-causing bacterium, virus, fungus or prion."}],"2016-Q15c":[{"q":"Which pairs a viral disease with a sign of it?","right":"Measles: red rash and high fever","wrong":["Tuberculosis: long-lasting cough","Common cold: rash all over the body","Polio: yellowing of skin and eyes"],"why":"Measles is viral and shows as a red rash with fever; TB is bacterial, and yellow skin is a sign of hepatitis."},{"q":"What is the biological basis of vaccination?","right":"It stimulates the body to make antibodies","wrong":["It gives the body ready-made antibodies","It kills pathogens already in the blood","It contains antibiotics that kill germs"],"why":"A vaccine's antigen stimulates your lymphocytes to make antibodies and memory cells: active immunity."}],"2017-Q4":[{"q":"What is meant by a pathogen?","right":"An organism that causes disease","wrong":["An organism that feeds on dead matter","An organism that makes its own food","An organism that needs no oxygen"],"why":"Pathogen means disease-causing; an organism that feeds on dead matter is a saprophyte, not a pathogen."},{"q":"What is an antibiotic?","right":"Microbe-made chemical that kills other microbes","wrong":["Body-made protein that targets antigens","Weakened microbe that gives immunity","Chemical that kills microbes on surfaces"],"why":"Antibiotics like penicillin are made by microbes (fungi or bacteria) and kill or stop the growth of other microbes."}],"2017-Q14a":[{"q":"Why were pregnant women told to avoid Zika areas?","right":"Zika may cause microcephaly in babies","wrong":["Pregnant women cannot make antibodies","Zika is quickly fatal to pregnant women","Antibiotics for Zika harm unborn babies"],"why":"The passage links Zika to microcephaly, where babies have small heads, so pregnant women were warned off."},{"q":"Which precaution helps prevent Zika spreading?","right":"Use nets and repellent against mosquitoes","wrong":["Take antibiotics before you travel","Drink only bottled water there","Go out mainly in the early morning"],"why":"Zika is spread by mosquito bites (peaking early morning and evening), so nets and repellent help; antibiotics don't."},{"q":"Which is an example of a beneficial virus?","right":"Bacteriophages, which kill bacteria","wrong":["Penicillium, which makes penicillin","Rhizobium, which fixes nitrogen","Yeast, which makes bread rise"],"why":"Bacteriophages are viruses that infect and kill bacteria; Penicillium and yeast are fungi and Rhizobium is a bacterium."},{"q":"After new virus parts are made in the host cell, what happens?","right":"They assemble and burst out of the cell","wrong":["They divide in two by binary fission","They form endospores inside the cell","They turn the host into a new virus"],"why":"New nucleic acid and coat proteins assemble into viruses, which are released, often by bursting the host cell."},{"q":"Which point supports viruses not being living?","right":"They can't reproduce on their own","wrong":["They have cells but no nucleus","They contain both DNA and RNA","They respire but do not grow"],"why":"Viruses are non-cellular, have no metabolism and only reproduce inside a host, so most say they're not alive."}],"2017-Q14b":[{"q":"Who is most at risk in a measles outbreak?","right":"People who were never vaccinated","wrong":["People who had measles as children","People fully vaccinated with MMR","People who recently took antibiotics"],"why":"Unvaccinated people (and babies too young for the jab) have no antibodies or memory cells for measles."},{"q":"What is a vaccine?","right":"A harmless dose of a pathogen or its antigen","wrong":["A dose of ready-made antibodies","A drug that kills the pathogen","A dose of donor white blood cells"],"why":"A vaccine is a weakened, dead or partial pathogen; its antigen makes your body produce antibodies and memory cells."},{"q":"Which list has three types of T lymphocyte?","right":"Killer, memory, suppressor","wrong":["Killer, helper, monocyte","Helper, B cell, killer","Suppressor, phagocyte, memory"],"why":"T cells come as helper, killer, suppressor and memory; B cells, monocytes and phagocytes are other white cells."},{"q":"What is the role of helper T cells?","right":"Stimulate B cells to make antibodies","wrong":["Burst infected cells with perforin","Switch off the immune response","Make antibodies against antigens"],"why":"Helper T cells recognise antigens and stimulate B cells and killer T cells; they don't make antibodies themselves."}],"2018-Q13a":[{"q":"Which entities contain only one type of nucleic acid?","right":"Viruses","wrong":["Bacteria","Prions","Yeasts"],"why":"A virus has either DNA or RNA, never both; bacteria and yeasts have both, and prions have none."}],"2018-Q15a":[{"q":"Why is overusing antibiotics dangerous?","right":"Resistant strains of bacteria build up","wrong":["Your body becomes immune to the drug","Viruses become resistant to them","They stop your body making antibodies"],"why":"Each course kills sensitive bacteria and leaves resistant ones to multiply, so the antibiotic stops working."}],"2018-Q15b":[{"q":"Why does active immunity last a long time?","right":"Your lymphocytes make memory cells","wrong":["Antibodies are given ready-made","Antibiotics stay in the blood for years","Antigens stay in the body for life"],"why":"Your own lymphocytes make antibodies and memory cells, which stay for years and respond fast to the same antigen."},{"q":"Why aren't antibiotics given to treat flu directly?","right":"Flu is a virus; antibiotics don't affect viruses","wrong":["Flu bacteria are all resistant already","Antibiotics would destroy flu antibodies","Antibiotics only work with a flu vaccine"],"why":"Influenza is a virus and antibiotics only act on bacteria (and fungi); they could also kill useful bacteria."}],"2019-Q14b":[{"q":"What is put into the body in vaccination?","right":"A weakened or dead form of the pathogen","wrong":["Ready-made antibodies from a donor","A live, full-strength pathogen","An antibiotic to kill the pathogen"],"why":"A vaccine is a harmless (weakened, dead or partial) pathogen; it triggers antibodies and memory cells without disease."},{"q":"Which can cause antibiotic resistance to build up?","right":"Not finishing a course of antibiotics","wrong":["Finishing every course of antibiotics","Getting vaccinated against the disease","Taking antibiotics only when needed"],"why":"Stopping early or overusing antibiotics leaves resistant bacteria alive to multiply and pass on resistance genes."}],"2020-Q15b":[{"q":"What are viral coats made of?","right":"Protein","wrong":["Lipid","Cellulose","Nucleic acid"],"why":"Viral coats (capsids) are protein, which is why HPV coat particles can act as antigens in the vaccine."},{"q":"Besides protein, what is the other main part of a virus?","right":"Nucleic acid","wrong":["Lipid","Carbohydrate","Amino acids"],"why":"A virus is protein plus nucleic acid, either DNA or RNA, which carries its genes; amino acids just build protein."},{"q":"Why are viruses obligate parasites?","right":"They can only reproduce in a host cell","wrong":["They can reproduce in soil or water","They must feed on dead material","They need oxygen to survive"],"why":"'Obligate' means they must have a host: a virus can only make copies of itself inside a living cell."},{"q":"How does a virus make its new parts inside the host?","right":"It uses the host cell's ribosomes and enzymes","wrong":["It uses its own ribosomes and enzymes","It splits in two by binary fission","It makes spores that grow into viruses"],"why":"A virus has no organelles, so its nucleic acid takes over the host's ribosomes and enzymes to make viral parts."},{"q":"What are the two types of induced immunity?","right":"Active and passive","wrong":["General and specific","Innate and inherited","B cell and T cell"],"why":"Induced immunity is active (you make antibodies) or passive (you get them); general and specific are defence systems."},{"q":"Which type of immunity follows the HPV vaccine?","right":"Active","wrong":["Passive","Innate","Inherited"],"why":"The vaccine's coat proteins are antigens, so your own body makes antibodies and memory cells: active immunity."},{"q":"How does active immunity differ from passive?","right":"Active immunity lasts much longer","wrong":["Active immunity works faster at first","Active immunity uses donated antibodies","Active immunity makes no memory cells"],"why":"Active immunity makes memory cells, so it lasts years; passive works at once but fades within weeks or months."}],"2021-Q16c":[{"q":"Where in the body are mucous membranes found?","right":"Lining the respiratory tract","wrong":["On the outer surface of skin","Lining the inside of arteries","Inside the bone marrow"],"why":"Mucous membranes line tubes open to the outside: the breathing, digestive and reproductive tracts."},{"q":"Where exactly are lymphocytes produced?","right":"Bone marrow","wrong":["Pancreas","Kidneys","Thyroid gland"],"why":"All lymphocytes are made in the bone marrow; T cells then move to the thymus to mature."},{"q":"Which type of lymphocyte produces antibodies?","right":"B lymphocytes","wrong":["Helper T cells","Killer T cells","Memory T cells"],"why":"B lymphocytes make antibodies; helper T cells only stimulate them, and killer T cells attack infected cells."},{"q":"Which white blood cell, other than a lymphocyte, fights infection?","right":"Macrophage","wrong":["Helper T cell","B lymphocyte","Platelet"],"why":"Macrophages (from monocytes) are phagocytes that engulf microbes; B and T cells are lymphocytes."},{"q":"From the graph, how does the response at B compare with A?","right":"B is faster and makes more antibodies","wrong":["B is slower and makes fewer antibodies","B is faster but makes fewer antibodies","B is slower but makes more antibodies"],"why":"After the vaccine (A) antibodies rose slowly and only a little; after infection (B) they rose fast and much higher."},{"q":"Why was the antibody response at B faster and bigger?","right":"Memory cells from the vaccine were present","wrong":["The person had no memory cells by B","The vaccine gave passive immunity","Antibiotics boosted antibodies at B"],"why":"The vaccine left memory cells, so the second meeting with the antigen got a fast, strong response."},{"q":"Which part of the virus do antibodies recognise?","right":"Proteins on its outer coat","wrong":["The RNA inside the coat","The virus's cell wall","The virus's ribosomes"],"why":"Antibodies bind antigens on the surface, which are coat proteins (like the spike); the RNA is hidden inside."},{"q":"Why can't antibiotics cure COVID-19?","right":"Antibiotics have no effect on viruses","wrong":["COVID-19 bacteria are all resistant","Antibiotics stop the vaccine working","The virus breaks down antibiotics"],"why":"COVID-19 is caused by a virus, and antibiotics act only on bacteria (and fungi), not viruses."}],"2022-Q5d":[{"q":"True or false: macrophages are white blood cells that secrete perforin.","right":"False","wrong":["True"],"why":"Macrophages engulf microbes by phagocytosis; perforin is released by killer T cells."}],"2022-Q7":[{"q":"What is molecule A on the diagram?","right":"Nucleic acid","wrong":["Protein","Lipid","Cellulose"],"why":"A is the genetic material inside the virus: a nucleic acid, which in SARS-CoV-2 is RNA."},{"q":"Where should the arrow from X point to show an antigen?","right":"A protein on the outer surface","wrong":["The RNA in the core of the virus","The inner side of the protein coat","The empty space inside the virus"],"why":"Antigens are the surface proteins (spikes) that antibodies recognise, so they're on the outside of the virus."},{"q":"What makes a virus an obligate parasite?","right":"It needs a host cell to reproduce","wrong":["It can reproduce by itself in water","It feeds on dead organic matter","It makes its own food from light"],"why":"Viruses have no machinery of their own, so they cannot replicate outside a living host cell."},{"q":"What does a vaccine do once it's in the body?","right":"Stimulates the body to make antibodies","wrong":["Kills the virus directly like a drug","Gives the body ready-made antibodies","Destroys infected cells with perforin"],"why":"A vaccine is a harmless dose of the pathogen or its antigen; it makes your body produce antibodies and memory cells."},{"q":"Which of these is a harmful virus?","right":"HIV","wrong":["MRSA","Salmonella","E. coli"],"why":"HIV is a virus that attacks the immune system; MRSA, Salmonella and harmful E. coli are all bacteria."},{"q":"Which is a beneficial use of a virus?","right":"Treating bacterial infections (phages)","wrong":["Making penicillin to treat infections","Brewing beer by fermentation","Breaking down sewage"],"why":"Phages are viruses that kill bacteria; penicillin, brewing and sewage treatment use fungi or bacteria instead."}],"2022-Q11b":[{"q":"Which of these is a harmful bacterium?","right":"Salmonella","wrong":["Influenza","Rhizobium","Penicillium"],"why":"Salmonella causes food poisoning; flu is a virus, Penicillium a fungus, and Rhizobium is a helpful bacterium."},{"q":"What is an antibiotic?","right":"A chemical that kills or slows bacteria","wrong":["A protein your body makes against antigens","A weakened bacterium that gives immunity","A chemical that kills viruses in cells"],"why":"Antibiotics kill or stop the growth of bacteria; they don't work on viruses, and antibodies are your own proteins."},{"q":"What is one reason antibiotic resistance has arisen?","right":"Overuse and misuse of antibiotics","wrong":["Too many people being vaccinated","Bacteria learning from antibodies","Antibiotics being used too rarely"],"why":"Overuse lets resistant mutants survive while the others die, so they multiply and take over."}],"2022-Q17b":[{"q":"Which is a biological benefit of breastfeeding?","right":"Gives a balanced supply of nutrients","wrong":["Gives the baby lifelong active immunity","Makes the mother ovulate sooner","Contains antibiotics that kill viruses"],"why":"Breast milk has the right balance of nutrients and passes on antibodies, which give short-term passive immunity."}],"2023-Q5e":[{"q":"Which of these is a harmful bacterium?","right":"Staphylococcus","wrong":["Rhizobium","Penicillium","Influenza"],"why":"Staphylococcus causes skin and wound infections (e.g. MRSA); Rhizobium is helpful, and the others aren't bacteria."}],"2023-Q6e":[{"q":"Why don't doctors give antibiotics for a viral infection?","right":"Antibiotics only kill bacteria and fungi","wrong":["Antibiotics make viruses multiply faster","Viruses need a higher dose of antibiotic","Antibiotics destroy the body's antibodies"],"why":"Antibiotics target bacteria (and fungi); viruses have none of the parts antibiotics attack, so they're unaffected."}],"2023-Q11b":[{"q":"What relationship is there between ash trees and the pathogen?","right":"Parasitism","wrong":["Mutualism","Competition","Predation"],"why":"The fungus lives on the ash tree and harms it, which is parasitism; in mutualism both partners benefit."},{"q":"How could ecologists help ash trees survive in Ireland?","right":"Keep a gene bank of resistant ash trees","wrong":["Vaccinate the ash trees against it","Plant more ash from continental Europe","Remove the moths that feed on ash"],"why":"Tolerant trees can be stored in a gene bank and bred for planting; imported plants brought the disease in."}],"2023-Q16c":[{"q":"On the diagram, what is structure X?","right":"Protein coat","wrong":["Nucleic acid","Cell wall","Cell membrane"],"why":"X is the outer protein coat (capsid); the nucleic acid, Y, is the genetic material inside it."},{"q":"Why is it hard to describe viruses as living?","right":"They have no metabolism of their own","wrong":["They are made of many cells","They contain no protein at all","They can grow and respire alone"],"why":"Viruses are non-cellular with no metabolism; they only show signs of life inside a host cell."},{"q":"Which of these diseases is caused by a virus?","right":"Hepatitis B","wrong":["Tetanus","Tuberculosis","Cholera"],"why":"Hepatitis B is a virus that attacks the liver; tetanus, TB and cholera are all caused by bacteria."},{"q":"How can viruses be useful to humans?","right":"To help make vaccines","wrong":["To make antibiotics","To ferment sugar into alcohol","To fix nitrogen in soil"],"why":"Viruses are used to make vaccines and as gene vectors; antibiotics, alcohol and nitrogen fixing use fungi or bacteria."},{"q":"Which of these is a SPECIFIC defence against viruses?","right":"Killer T cells destroy infected cells","wrong":["Skin acts as a barrier to entry","Mucus traps the virus particles","Phagocytes engulf infected cells"],"why":"Killer T cells target one specific antigen; skin, mucus and phagocytes act against any pathogen, so they're general."}],"2024-Q14b":[{"q":"What does 'pathogenic' mean?","right":"Able to cause disease","wrong":["Able to form endospores","Able to make antibiotics","Able to fix nitrogen"],"why":"A pathogenic bacterium causes disease; forming endospores or making antibiotics doesn't make it a pathogen."},{"q":"What can misusing antibiotics lead to?","right":"Antibiotic-resistant bacteria","wrong":["Antibiotic-resistant viruses","Lifelong immunity to bacteria","A lack of antibodies in blood"],"why":"Misuse kills sensitive bacteria and leaves resistant ones to multiply, so infections get harder to treat."}],"2024-Q14c":[{"q":"Why is it hard to describe viruses as living organisms?","right":"They are not made of cells","wrong":["They are made of a single cell","They have DNA but no protein","They feed on dead organic matter"],"why":"Viruses aren't cells and have no metabolism, so they break the rule that living things are made of cells."},{"q":"Which two biochemicals make up all viruses?","right":"Protein and nucleic acid","wrong":["Lipid and carbohydrate","Cellulose and chitin","Phospholipid and starch"],"why":"Every virus is a protein coat around nucleic acid (DNA or RNA); some also have a fatty envelope, but not all."},{"q":"Which is the right order for viral replication?","right":"Attach, inject, copy, assemble, release","wrong":["Inject, attach, assemble, copy, release","Attach, copy, inject, release, assemble","Attach, inject, release, copy, assemble"],"why":"A virus attaches, injects its nucleic acid, uses the host to copy its parts, assembles new viruses and releases them."},{"q":"Which of these is a harmful virus?","right":"Coronavirus","wrong":["Streptococcus","Listeria","Penicillium"],"why":"Coronaviruses cause colds and COVID-19; Streptococcus and Listeria are bacteria and Penicillium is a fungus."},{"q":"Which is a way viruses can be beneficial?","right":"As vectors to carry genes into cells","wrong":["As a source of penicillin","As decomposers in the soil","By fixing nitrogen for plants"],"why":"Viruses naturally carry genes into cells, so they're used as vectors in genetic engineering and gene therapy."}],"2024-Q16d":[{"q":"Which is a method of the GENERAL defence system?","right":"Phagocytosis by white blood cells","wrong":["Antibody production by B cells","Killer T cells bursting cells","Memory cells after a vaccine"],"why":"General defences act against any microbe, like skin, acid and phagocytosis; antibodies and T cells are specific."},{"q":"Which organ is specific to the immune system?","right":"Spleen","wrong":["Liver","Pancreas","Thyroid"],"why":"The spleen filters blood and holds lymphocytes; the liver, pancreas and thyroid have other jobs."},{"q":"Which sentence correctly describes an antigen?","right":"A molecule that triggers antibody production","wrong":["A protein made by B cells to fight germs","A drug made by microbes to kill bacteria","A harmless dose of a dead pathogen"],"why":"An antigen (e.g. on a microbe's surface) triggers the response; antibodies are the proteins made against it."},{"q":"Besides T cells, what is the other type of lymphocyte?","right":"B cells","wrong":["Monocytes","Macrophages","Platelets"],"why":"Lymphocytes are B cells and T cells; monocytes and macrophages are phagocytes, and platelets help clotting."},{"q":"What is the role of memory T cells?","right":"Remember an antigen for a faster response","wrong":["Burst infected cells using perforin","Switch off the immune response","Make antibodies to the antigen"],"why":"Memory T cells stay for years and remember the antigen, so a second infection is fought off quickly."}],"2025-Q16d":[{"q":"Which is an organ of the lymphatic system?","right":"Tonsils","wrong":["Thyroid","Kidneys","Adrenals"],"why":"Tonsils, lymph nodes, the thymus and the spleen are lymphatic organs; the thyroid and adrenals are hormone glands."},{"q":"Which is a job of the lymphatic system?","right":"Returns tissue fluid to the blood","wrong":["Carries oxygen to the tissues","Makes bile to digest fats","Pumps blood around the body"],"why":"Lymph vessels return leaked tissue fluid to the blood and carry absorbed fats; red blood cells carry oxygen."},{"q":"Where exactly in the body are lymphocytes made?","right":"Bone marrow","wrong":["Pancreas","Kidneys","Thyroid gland"],"why":"Lymphocytes are made in the bone marrow; the thymus is only where T cells go to mature."},{"q":"Which matches a lymphocyte type to its role?","right":"Killer T cell: destroys infected cells","wrong":["B cell: engulfs microbes by phagocytosis","Killer T cell: makes antibodies","Helper T cell: switches off response"],"why":"Killer T cells burst infected cells; B cells make antibodies and suppressor T cells switch the response off."},{"q":"What is vaccination?","right":"Giving a harmless antigen to trigger immunity","wrong":["Giving antibodies for instant immunity","Giving antibiotics to prevent infection","Giving a full dose of a live pathogen"],"why":"Vaccination brings in a weakened or harmless pathogen or antigen so your body builds active immunity."}],"2026-Q16b":[{"q":"Which pair names X and Y on diagram 1?","right":"X protein, Y nucleic acid","wrong":["X nucleic acid, Y protein","X lipid, Y protein","X lipid, Y carbohydrate"],"why":"X is the protein coat and Y is the genetic material inside it, DNA or RNA; every virus has both."},{"q":"Apart from round, what other shape can a virus be?","right":"Rod-shaped","wrong":["Comma-shaped","Chain of spheres","Grape-like cluster"],"why":"Viruses can be round, rod-shaped or complex (like phages); comma, chain and cluster shapes describe bacteria."},{"q":"In diagram 2, how does the virus use the host cell?","right":"Uses its organelles to copy viral parts","wrong":["Splits in two inside it by binary fission","Uses its own ribosomes inside the cell","Forms spores inside it that later hatch"],"why":"Viral DNA or RNA enters and uses the host's organelles and materials to make viral parts, which assemble and burst out."},{"q":"Why is a red blood cell a poor host for viruses?","right":"It has no nucleus or organelles","wrong":["It has a thick cell wall","It is full of antibodies","Its nucleus destroys viral DNA"],"why":"A mature red cell has no nucleus or organelles, so a virus has no machinery to hijack for copying itself."},{"q":"Which of these is caused by a harmful virus?","right":"Rabies","wrong":["Cholera","Whooping cough","Thrush"],"why":"Rabies is caused by a virus; cholera and whooping cough are bacterial, and thrush is caused by a fungus."},{"q":"Which is a beneficial use of viruses?","right":"Gene therapy: carrying genes into cells","wrong":["Producing penicillin for medicine","Turning milk into cheese","Decomposing dead leaves"],"why":"Viruses can deliver genes into cells for gene therapy; penicillin, cheese and decay rely on fungi or bacteria."}],"S1-Q3b":[{"q":"What is the main role of monocytes in innate immunity?","right":"Engulf and digest pathogens (phagocytosis)","wrong":["Make antibodies against one antigen","Burst infected cells using perforin","Remember antigens for next time"],"why":"Monocytes (macrophages) engulf any pathogen or dust and digest it with lysosome enzymes: a non-specific defence."},{"q":"Which is another innate (non-specific) response?","right":"Inflammation brings more phagocytes to a site","wrong":["B cells making antibodies to an antigen","Memory cells forming after a vaccine","Killer T cells attacking a virus"],"why":"In inflammation, histamine widens blood vessels so more phagocytes arrive; antibodies and T cells are specific."}],"S2-Q1b":[{"q":"Why test the vaccine on people from all over the world?","right":"Different populations may respond differently","wrong":["So that no placebo group was needed","To make the trial double-blind","To reduce the number of people needed"],"why":"Genes and pathogen strains vary between regions, so a worldwide sample shows whether it works for everyone."},{"q":"What does 'placebo-controlled' mean here?","right":"One group got a dummy injection to compare","wrong":["Everyone got the vaccine, at different doses","No one knew which group they were in","The placebo group got antibodies instead"],"why":"The placebo group gets an identical injection with no vaccine, so any difference is down to the vaccine itself."},{"q":"How does a sample of 43 448 people affect reliability?","right":"A large sample makes the data more reliable","wrong":["A large sample makes the data less reliable","Sample size has no effect on reliability","Only the number of doses affects reliability"],"why":"A big sample cuts out the effect of chance and odd individual results, so the findings are more reliable."},{"q":"Can we conclude the vaccine works for under-16s?","right":"No: only people 16 or over were tested","wrong":["Yes: 95% protection applies to everyone","Yes: children have stronger immune systems","Yes: the sample size was very large"],"why":"The trial only included people aged 16 or over, so you can't extend the result to children without testing them."}],"S2-Q1e":[{"q":"Why can prions be called pathogens?","right":"They cause disease, such as BSE and CJD","wrong":["They are living cells that reproduce","They contain DNA that infects cells","They are a type of virus"],"why":"A pathogen is anything that causes disease; prions are misfolded proteins that cause BSE and CJD."},{"q":"How could BSE prions pass from cattle to humans?","right":"Eating beef from infected cattle","wrong":["Mosquito bites after biting cattle","Breathing in air near infected cattle","Touching the skin of infected cattle"],"why":"vCJD came from eating infected beef products, especially brain or spinal cord tissue where prions build up."},{"q":"Why does the way a protein folds matter?","right":"It gives the shape the protein needs to work","wrong":["It stores the protein's genetic code","It joins the amino acids together","It stops the protein ever denaturing"],"why":"Folding gives the 3D shape (like an active site); the wrong shape means the protein can't do its job."},{"q":"Which other pathogen is non-cellular?","right":"Virus","wrong":["Bacterium","Fungus","Protist"],"why":"Viruses, like prions, aren't made of cells; bacteria, fungi and protists are all cellular."}]};
